<Project>

  <!--
    PRIV-PDBPATH (REL.0A): no absolute development path in a Release binary.

    By default the compiler records where the build ran: the PE debug directory carries the full PDB
    path (the CodeView entry), every CallerFilePath constant is the full source path, the PDB's
    documents and its Source Link map name the checkout folder. For a checkout that lives under a user
    profile, that is the user name and the folder layout of the machine, shipped inside the MSIX.

    ContinuousIntegrationBuild turns on the SDK's documented deterministic-source-paths mode
    (DeterministicSourcePaths): every SourceRoot, i.e. the git checkout, is mapped to /_/ through
    PathMap, and Source Link is written against the mapped path, so the PDB still resolves to the
    exact commit on GitHub. It applies to every project under src/ - App, WidgetProvider (including
    the provider publish the packaged App build runs), the two contracts, Core, Features,
    Infrastructure, Collectors - and to the code the XAML compiler and source generators put in obj/,
    which lives under the same root.

    Release only, on every machine, not only on CI: the leak was found in a Release MSIX built on a
    developer machine for QA, and any Release package can end up shared. Debug keeps real local
    paths, so breakpoints and source stepping are unchanged. Tests are deliberately not covered: they
    are never distributed, and tools/release/Assert-DistributionPrivacy.ps1 checks the package anyway.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild>true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
